-- usage --

import LLMRenderer and the style.css entrypoint:

  import { LLMRenderer } from 'vue-llm-ui'
  import 'vue-llm-ui/style.css'

you must import the css or block components and markdown will have no styling.

bind a reactive string to :text and append to it as the LLM streams. the
component re-parses on every change. there is no "stream mode" toggle - just
keep appending characters to the ref.

register block types by passing an array of { type, component } objects to
the :blocks prop. the type string must exactly match what the LLM emits in
the JSON payload's "type" field.

-- block components --

every block component receives a single prop called "block" which is the
full parsed JSON object from inside the delimiters. the entire object is
passed, not just a subset. so if the LLM emits:

  【{type:"chart", chart:"pie", datasetId:"abc-123", title:"Fuel Costs"}】

your component gets:

  props.block = {
    type: "chart",
    chart: "pie",
    datasetId: "abc-123",
    title: "Fuel Costs"
  }

you define the shape. the renderer doesn't validate or transform it. whatever
the LLM puts in the JSON, your component receives as-is (after json repair).

block components are regular vue SFCs. they can fetch data, have local state,
emit events - whatever you need. the renderer just mounts them with the block
prop and gets out of the way.

-- block delimiters --

blocks use full-width bracket characters: 【 and 】 (U+3010 and U+3011).
these are NOT regular square brackets. the LLM must emit these exact unicode
characters. in your system prompt, include a clear example so the model knows
to use them.

the JSON inside does not need to be strict - unquoted keys, trailing commas,
single quotes all get auto-repaired. this matters because LLMs often produce
sloppy JSON mid-stream. don't try to enforce strict JSON in your system prompt,
it causes more problems than it solves.

-- streaming behavior --

during streaming, if the opening 【 has been emitted but the closing 】 hasn't
arrived yet, the renderer shows a loading state via GenericBlockComponent. once
the closing delimiter arrives, it attempts to parse and render.

if jsonrepair can't fix the JSON (truly broken), it falls back to an error
state showing the raw content. this is rare in practice.

whitespace-only text between blocks is discarded. if you need spacing between
blocks, use markdown (a blank line or a heading) rather than relying on
whitespace.

-- code blocks --

block delimiters inside markdown code fences (triple backticks) are ignored by
the parser. this means the LLM can safely show block syntax examples in code
without them being parsed as real blocks.

inline backtick code also suppresses parsing - a block delimiter inside `inline
code` won't be treated as a block.

-- shiki / syntax highlighting --

shiki is a dependency and gets installed automatically. the highlighter is
initialized lazily on first render via a singleton promise. if it hasn't loaded
yet when a code block appears, it falls back to vue-markdown-render.

pass a theme name via the :theme prop on LLMRenderer. default is "rose-pine".
available: rose-pine, rose-pine-moon, rose-pine-dawn, vitesse-dark,
vitesse-light, github-dark, github-light, dracula, nord, one-dark-pro.

supported languages for highlighting: javascript, typescript, python, json,
markdown, html, css, vue. if you need more languages, call getShikiHighlighter()
and load them yourself before mounting the component.

-- prompt engineering --

tell the LLM about your registered block types in the system prompt. be
explicit about the delimiter characters and the expected JSON shape. example:

  "when presenting data visually, emit a block using full-width brackets:
   【{type:"chart", chart:"bar", datasetId:"<id>", title:"<title>"}】
   available types: chart, table, metric-card"

keep the JSON payloads small. don't put datasets inline - use an ID reference
and have the block component fetch the data. the LLM should call a tool to
store query results, get back an ID, and pass that ID in the block payload.

-- pitfalls --

the renderer uses v-bind="chunk.props" which means your block component cannot
have a prop named "is" or "key" - these conflict with vue's special attributes.

LLMRenderer's styles are scoped. if you want to style the gap between rendered
chunks or override markdown typography, target .llm-renderer from an unscoped
parent.

GenericBlockComponent has hardcoded light-theme colors. if your app is dark
themed, you'll want to override its styles or replace it entirely.

the entire text prop is re-parsed on every update. for very long conversations
(10k+ characters with many blocks), this could become a performance concern.
for typical chatbot responses (a few hundred to a few thousand chars) it's fine.
